+\paragraph{Interpreted quadratic arithmetic completion (Block 21).}
+Fix the continuous quotient
+\[
+q:G_{\Q}\longrightarrow C_2
+\]
+obtained by the action on a chosen square root $i$ of $-1$ in the algebraic
+closure. Its surjectivity and the equivalence
+\[
+q(\sigma)=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\sigma(i)=i
+\]
+are proved using the existing actual quadratic Galois construction.
+For a commutative ring $B$, define the arithmetic packet
+\[
+\mathcal P_q(B)=
+\{\chi:G_{\Q}\longrightarrow B^\times\mid\ker q\subseteq\ker\chi\},
+\]
+where the maps are group homomorphisms. Unique descent through the actual
+surjective map $q$ is derived, rather than supplied. Every member has finite
+image and is continuous for the coefficient topology, since its descent
+has the finite discrete domain $C_2$.
+
+Let $R$ be any commutative ring and $B$ any commutative $R$-algebra. The
+coordinate ring $A_R=R[X]/(X^2-1)$ represents precisely this specified
+packet:
+\[
+\Hom_{R\text{-alg}}(A_R,B)
+\simeq\{x\in B:x^2=1\}
+\simeq\Hom(C_2,B^\times)
+\simeq\mathcal P_q(B).
+\]
+The first map evaluates the coordinate. Its inverse is the quotient
+universal property. A root is a unit with itself as inverse and determines
+a character by its value on the generator of $C_2$. Finally pull back
+along $q$. All inverse identities and naturality under coefficient
+homomorphisms are proved. In particular, the point reductions and actual
+arithmetic character reductions coincide. There is no invertibility-of-two,
+reducedness or nontriviality hypothesis on $B$. No smoothness or
+\'{e}taleness statement at two is used or asserted.
+
+Let $I\subseteq R$ and suppose that $R$ is $I$-adically complete. Using
+the checked ring-map reconstruction on the quadratic coordinate ring,
+Lean proves
+\[
+\mathcal P_q(R)\simeq\varprojlim_{m\ge0}\mathcal P_q(R/I^m).
+\]
+The zero-precision quotient is included only for the formal indexing.
+The inverse constructs an actual arithmetic character, with exactly the
+prescribed reductions. It is unique, and its continuity is derived from
+its factorization through the same finite quotient.
+
+There is also a completion theorem without chosen compatible characters.
+Suppose that $R/I^m$ is finite for every $m\ge1$, and that
+$C_m\subseteq\mathcal P_q(R/I^m)$ are allowed subsets such that every
+reduction of an allowed member is allowed. Then
+\[
+\begin{split}
+&\exists\chi\in\mathcal P_q(R),\quad
+  \chi\text{ is continuous and }\chi\bmod I^m\in C_m
+  \text{ for all }m\ge1\\
+&\hspace{3em}\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+  C_m\ne\varnothing\text{ for all }m\ge1.
+\end{split}
+\]
+The root interpretation makes the allowed finite levels genuinely finite.
+Finite-branch compactness chooses the compatible roots, and the ring
+reconstruction gives their integral root. Naturality turns this into the
+required arithmetic character. Surjectivity of transitions and individual
+liftability of every finite node are not assumed. The root-level theorem
+needs only the allowed root subsets to be finite.
+
+For $R=\Z_2$, Lean derives finiteness of all positive maximal-ideal
+quotients and applies this completion theorem. Since $\Z_2$ is a domain,
+its only roots of $X^2-1$ are $1$ and $-1$. Thus the integral packet has
+exactly two distinct characters: the trivial character and the sign
+character describing the action on $i$. They have the same reduction
+modulo two and distinct reductions modulo four.
+
+The distinction between finite occupancy and continuation is witnessed
+by an actual finite arithmetic character. The roots modulo eight are
+$1,3,5,7$. The root $3$ defines a member of
+$\mathcal P_q(\Z/8\Z)$, but no member of
+$\mathcal P_q(\Z/16\Z)$ reduces to it. Indeed the only scalar lifts of
+$3$ are $3$ and $11$, and both square to $9$ modulo sixteen. The formal
+proof also classifies all roots modulo sixteen and the reductions with
+children. Consequently a nonempty finite node need not lie on an
+infinite branch, even in this explicitly interpreted arithmetic packet.
+
+Block 21 completes this restricted arithmetic interpretation and its
+conditional completion. The packet is defined by one fixed quotient; it
+is not the collection of all quadratic characters of $G_{\Q}$. No local
+ramification criterion, Hodge--Tate weight, Weil--Deligne attachment, or
+cross-coefficient local invariant is established here. The full
+manuscript still requires its remaining packet identifications and
+arithmetic comparisons. The ledger remains fifteen checked claims and
+five partial claims, and universal positive occupancy remains conjectural.
